\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Neither budget is neutral. Evaluation cost spans a factor of $199$ across the configurations (left), so at equal wall-clock the cheaper ones complete far more search (right). Both quantities are reported for every cell.}}{23}{figure.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocol}{{3}{23}{Neither budget is neutral. Evaluation cost spans a factor of $199$ across the configurations (left), so at equal wall-clock the cheaper ones complete far more search (right). Both quantities are reported for every cell}{figure.caption.19}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The same four comparisons under both budget conventions, against the full closed loop. Equal generations does not charge for the extra work a mechanism performs, and systematically flatters it; the gap between the two markers in a row is the part of the apparent gain that is an accounting artefact. Measured on the four instances for which both protocols were run.}}{24}{figure.caption.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocols}{{4}{24}{The same four comparisons under both budget conventions, against the full closed loop. Equal generations does not charge for the extra work a mechanism performs, and systematically flatters it; the gap between the two markers in a row is the part of the apparent gain that is an accounting artefact. Measured on the four instances for which both protocols were run}{figure.caption.20}{}}
